23:12:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an excellent point, and it shows you’re really thinking like a scientist — comparing biological systems and asking why a rule that works for one animal wouldn't work for us. You’re right: lobsters, horseshoe crabs, and many mollusks have **blue blood** because they use a copper-based protein called **hemocyanin** to carry oxygen, instead of our iron-based hemoglobin. But the parallel actually helps explain why our blood *doesn't* turn blue, once you look closely at the chemistry. Let’s break it down. ### First, a crucial correction about the “blue blood” of lobsters Here’s something surprising: **lobster blood is not always blue.** - When hemocyanin is **deoxygenated** (like in a vein returning to the gills), it is actually **colorless or very pale**. Copper in the deoxy state is Cu⁺, which doesn’t produce visible color in that protein environment. - When hemocyanin **picks up oxygen**, the copper goes to Cu²⁺ and the protein turns a striking **blue**. So their blood is *blue when oxygenated*, not when deoxygenated. This is the exact opposite of the myth about human veins. If our blood followed the lobster pattern, our **arteries** would be blue, and our veins would be pale — but both would be weirdly colored. The “blue = deoxygenated” idea doesn’t match the actual animal model you’re citing. ### Why doesn’t our iron do something similar? You’re asking, “If copper can make blood blue, why can’t iron make it blue when oxygen is low?” The answer lies in the fact that the color depends not just on the metal atom, but on the *entire chemical structure* around it — the protein and the ring system holding the metal. - **Hemoglobin’s iron sits inside a heme group** (a porphyrin ring). That ring strongly dictates what light gets absorbed. Both oxygenated and deoxygenated hemoglobin absorb heavily in the green-yellow part of the spectrum, but they reflect red light. - Oxyhemoglobin reflects a **bright cherry red**. - Deoxyhemoglobin reflects a **dark maroon/burgundy** — still red, just much less intense. The difference is due to a slight shift in the electronic structure of the iron-heme complex, but it never produces a blue reflection. Essentially, the heme ring “flavors” the iron’s color to stay in the red zone, no matter if O₂ is bound or not. - **To get blue blood from iron**, you’d need a totally different protein architecture. For example, some iron compounds *can* be blue (think Prussian blue, an iron-cyanide pigment). But that’s a completely different chemical environment — not just deoxygenation. If our bodies used a copper-based system like hemocyanin, the blood would be blue only when oxygenated, and colorless when deoxygenated. That’s not what we see in veins either. ### So why did the myth sound plausible? Probably because people made a logical jump: *Copper = blue blood in animals. Iron = red blood in us. But maybe when iron loses oxygen, it switches to a different color, like a chameleon, and blue seemed a natural guess.* Your biology teacher likely absorbed this intuition, combined it with the blue appearance of veins through skin, and taught it as fact. But the chemistry just doesn’t support it. Deoxygenated hemoglobin is not blue under any circumstances, whether inside your body or in a test tube without air. If you were to look at a vein directly (surgically), it would appear dark reddish-purple, never blue. Your comparison to lobsters actually strengthens the case against the myth. It shows you’re thinking about biochemistry on the right track — you just needed that extra detail about *when* hemocyanin is blue. So yes, your teacher was wrong, but your reasoning about parallel systems is exactly what makes for good science.
